Dear Amina,

Thank you for the field notes from Kakuma. On your question: I do not think health-related calls should be used to train the chatbot in the first phase.

Many of these calls mention diagnoses, pregnancies or HIV status. Even with names removed, a caller can often be recognised from the clinic, the date and the condition. Our clinic register links calls to patient records, for example MRN 448-1207 or MRN 512-0033, and those numbers appear in some transcripts.

If the team still wants to include health calls later, I would ask for three things:
1. Separate, explicit consent for health data, asked by a clinician, not by the helpline.
2. Removal of medical record numbers, clinic names and dates of visits, not only names and phone numbers.
3. An ethics committee review before any use.

For the record, the patient who called last week about her referral (Mary Achieng, born 14 June 1994, phone +254 722 555 918) asked that her call not be stored at all. Please make sure it is deleted.

I am happy to join the next working group meeting. You can reach me at the clinic on +254 20 555 0147.

Best regards,
Dr. Samuel Otieno
Medical coordinator, Northbridge Relief Network (fictional), Kakuma clinic
